#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153052} :activity/id #uuid "96a53612-ec02-52e7-9961-40c187e6e800" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050883 :topic/id #uuid "6e302473-4377-5624-b6bf-63d1436ec9f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2615} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151941 :question/id #uuid "150649f4-8b1e-563a-a774-1857d8781212" :question/math-academy-id "e-6247"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41a9d47a-2dd6-5177-afd1-e4adcd887f55" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049133}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544856}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "30e60cbf-4794-5b51-be6a-93b451386c0a" :step/math-academy-id 23452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151942 :question/id #uuid "3923e1d8-73e5-501c-96b5-d6b729260d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6249"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd33f89c-3adc-5b57-b7b7-4877a9e54e11" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544787}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "5f845909-7a57-5bcc-b7ec-58d8bd3178cd" :step/math-academy-id 23454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153186}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153351 :tutorial/content "We know how to write the lower Riemann sum for a function $f(x,y)$ over a *fixed* rectangular partition. In this lesson, we will learn how to write down an expression for the lower Riemann sum for an *arbitrary* rectangular partition. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $R = [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and define the function $f(x,y)$ as \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nFurther, let $P$ be an arbitrary partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find an expression for $L(f, P),$ the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition. We proceed identically to the case where $P$ was fixed.\n\nFirst, we consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{j-1}$ and $y_j$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nSince we're computing the *lower* sum, we're interested in the *minimum* value of $f$ in this subregion.\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $x$ increases on $[0,1],$ and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ increases as $y$ increases on $[0,1].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nTherefore, the *minimum* value of $f$ in the subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value of $f$ at the *bottom left corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nSo, if $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion, we have\n\n$$\nm_{ij} = f(x_{i-1}, y_{j-1}) = x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\leq i\\leq m$ and $1\\leq j\\leq n.$ \n\nSince the area of $R_{ij}$ is $\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j,$ the volume of the rectangular solid with base $R_{ij}$ and height $m_{ij}$ is\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\Delta x_i\\Delta y_j = (x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$\n\nFinally, summing up all of our rectangular solids over the entire partition $P$, we find that the lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = \\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^n (x_{i-1}^2 + y_{j-1}^2)\\Delta x_i \\Delta y_j \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ef8305a-8a99-57ba-8aee-063624e8571a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3958 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e1b4e0b9-1f1d-5d68-a2bc-6a50156f08de" :step/math-academy-id 23479 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152232 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152042 :question/id #uuid "b3b3c2bc-029c-5128-8ea9-00591c6936d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9496"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "afd97f63-933b-5595-8b4d-01f14c3bff53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544875}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Function That's Neither Strictly Increasing Nor Decreasing"} :step/id #uuid "85ace4fd-3ba9-514e-a57a-7f919b2ae2a0" :step/math-academy-id 35555}] :activity/title "Lower Riemann Sums Over General Rectangular Partitions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152327}]}
